WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.831
In 1609 Galileo turned his telescope to the heavens.

2
00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:10.092
He looked at Jupiter and was astonished; he saw four moons orbiting the planet.

3
00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:15.058
As the centuries passed, people invented larger and more powerful telescopes.

4
00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:21.463
But these improvements could not satisfy their curiosity about the other planets that orbit our sun.

5
00:00:21.464 --> 00:00:27.967
Instead of improving telescopes on Earth, scientists thought, why not send telescopes to the planets?

6
00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:34.292
In 1977 the U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) sent two probes,

7
00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:40.196
Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, to explore the mysterious planets beyond Mars.

8
00:00:40.197 --> 00:00:47.946
These four planets are called gas giants, and they are about 60 to 1,300 times the size of Earth.

9
00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:53.335
Both Voyager 1 and 2 explored the closest two, Jupiter and Saturn.

10
00:00:53.336 --> 00:01:01.621
Voyager 1 also studied Saturn's moon Titan, while Voyager 2 traveled on to study the planets Uranus and Neptune.

11
00:01:01.622 --> 00:01:11.595
From January to April of 1979, Voyager 1 photographed and studied Jupiter and its many moons (scientists keep discovering new ones).

12
00:01:11.596 --> 00:01:18.843
Beautiful pictures were sent to Earth, allowing scientists to research the complicated weather systems that wrap around the planet.

13
00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:26.480
Jupiter's atmosphere to core temperatures can range from -145 to +24,000 degrees Celsius.

14
00:01:26.481 --> 00:01:33.707
Jupiter has lightning and its own auroral phenomenon, just like Earth's northern lights.

15
00:01:33.708 --> 00:01:40.141
When images of one of Jupiter's moons, Io, were sent back to Earth, scientists were astonished.

16
00:01:40.142 --> 00:01:45.623
Moons, it was believed, could not have volcanoes because they were too small and cold.

17
00:01:45.624 --> 00:01:53.783
But Io has volcanoes more violent than any on Earth, with eruptions that send material three hundred kilometers high.

18
00:01:53.784 --> 00:01:57.369
Later, scientists realized that because Jupiter is so big,

19
00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:03.287
its gravity pulls the side of the moon closest to Jupiter more strongly than the opposite side.

20
00:02:03.288 --> 00:02:09.158
As Io rotates, its different parts are constantly stretched and pulled, and this creates heat.

21
00:02:09.159 --> 00:02:16.992
For example, if you take a metal spoon and bend it back and forth, the place where it bends becomes hot.

22
00:02:16.993 --> 00:02:24.710
Jupiter's gravity is so powerful that it bends the interior of its moons. Scientists call this process tidal heating.

23
00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:32.086
On Jupiter's moon Io, tidal heating is powerful enough to cause eruptions hundreds of times larger than any volcano on Earth.

24
00:02:32.087 --> 00:02:38.517
The Voyager probes also discovered that Jupiter's moon Europa has a surface of ice.

25
00:02:38.518 --> 00:02:47.932
If tidal heating warms Europa's interior enough to melt the ice, is it possible that life exists in vast oceans underneath its icy surface?

26
00:02:47.933 --> 00:02:53.156
Scientists are now designing probes to land on Europa to answer this question.

27
00:02:53.157 --> 00:03:00.471
From Earth, Saturn and its rings appear peaceful and beautiful, but the Voyager probes discovered something different.

28
00:03:00.472 --> 00:03:09.585
Saturn has violent windstorms over 1,400 kilometers per hour, and its rings are not the perfectly smooth objects we see through telescopes.

29
00:03:09.586 --> 00:03:13.485
Some rings have gaps, like a necklace missing a few pearls.

30
00:03:13.486 --> 00:03:18.163
But strangest of all, the rings have patterns in them that look like braided hair.

31
00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:26.388
Scientists believe that the uneven orbits of Saturn's small moons cause these strange patterns, and the gaps as well.

32
00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:31.530
Voyager 1 also studied Titan, a large moon that orbits Saturn.

33
00:03:31.531 --> 00:03:35.048
Titan is the only moon in the solar system to have an atmosphere.

34
00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:42.274
Voyager 1 found that Titan's atmosphere is made mostly of nitrogen, while the surface is a mixture of ice and rock.

35
00:03:42.275 --> 00:03:47.554
Before there was life on Earth, its atmosphere was also mostly nitrogen.

36
00:03:47.555 --> 00:03:53.299
Some scientists believe that when we look at Titan, we are seeing what Earth looked like far in the past.

37
00:03:53.300 --> 00:04:00.099
In 1986 Voyager 2 arrived at Uranus and discovered a shocking mystery that is unexplained to this day.

38
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:04.026
Most planets, like Earth, spin on their axes vertically.

39
00:04:04.027 --> 00:04:10.818
Their north and south poles point up and down compared to the rest of the solar system.

40
00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:19.398
Uranus is the opposite. Its axis lines up horizontally, so that one pole faces toward the sun and the other away from the sun.

41
00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:25.076
This strange fact had long been known, but Voyager 2 discovered a stranger contradiction.

42
00:04:25.077 --> 00:04:28.784
Even though the equator of Uranus receives almost no sunlight,

43
00:04:28.785 --> 00:04:35.855
its equator is always as hot as, or hotter than, the polar region that directly faces the sun.

44
00:04:35.856 --> 00:04:44.829
Voyager 2 arrived at Neptune in 1989. It discovered six moons orbiting Neptune, as well as a faint ring system around the planet.

45
00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:53.842
Neptune is a beautiful world, a deep blue color with giant white clouds of methane rising and falling, just as water does in Earth's atmosphere.

46
00:04:53.843 --> 00:04:59.985
After its mission to Neptune, Voyager 2 began to travel out of the solar system.

47
00:04:59.986 --> 00:05:05.953
The success of the Voyager missions encouraged scientists to send more probes to explore our solar system.

48
00:05:05.954 --> 00:05:12.109
The Cassini-Huygens spaceship, after traveling for seven years, is now orbiting and studying Saturn.

49
00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:16.058
In 2004 NASA landed two rovers on Mars.

50
00:05:16.059 --> 00:05:23.777
These small, six-wheeled, solar-powered vehicles are sending back pictures and doing tests on Martian rocks.

51
00:05:23.778 --> 00:05:27.097
Since the Voyager probes were launched in 1977,

52
00:05:27.098 --> 00:05:32.794
NASA, the European Space Agency, and the Japanese Aerospace Exploration Agency

53
00:05:32.795 --> 00:05:40.281
have sent more than 35 new missions to explore the inner and outer planets, the moon, asteroids, comets, and the sun.

54
00:05:40.282 --> 00:05:46.585
Today both Voyager 1 and 2 are on their way out of the solar system.

55
00:05:46.586 --> 00:05:53.695
Voyager 1 is over 16 billion kilometers from Earth and is the most distant man-made object in the universe.

56
00:05:53.696 --> 00:05:57.069
But the missions of these pioneering probes continue.

57
00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:05.643
They are now studying the complex area of space where our solar system ends and the rest of the universe begins.

58
00:06:05.644 --> 00:06:13.677
In addition to their scientific missions, Voyager 1 and 2 may play a much more important role in humanity's exploration of space.

59
00:06:13.678 --> 00:06:18.062
Both probes carry a gold-plated disc containing messages from Earth.

60
00:06:18.063 --> 00:06:20.408
If aliens ever find a Voyager probe,

61
00:06:20.409 --> 00:06:28.817
they will be able to hear the natural sounds of our planet, a selection of music, and greetings in over fifty languages.

62
00:06:28.818 --> 00:06:32.395
But perhaps the most important message is not on the discs at all.

63
00:06:32.396 --> 00:06:38.678
Both spaceships are themselves a statement about one of humanity's best qualities: curiosity.

64
00:06:38.679 --> 00:06:45.993
Like Galileo, many of us on Earth want to see farther and understand what we see.

